#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180488} :activity/id #uuid "081747a0-57ee-5aec-8eb5-e43238a80d3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916 :topic/id #uuid "cbfc5fbb-c887-5448-b39f-e7818405fa55" :topic/math-academy-id 3711} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180718 :tutorial/content "The line integral of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf F$ along a piecewise smooth curve $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r .\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from an initial point $A$ to a terminal point $B$ along the curve.\n\nIf the curve can be parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then we can calculate the line integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt\n$$\n\nOften, the trickiest part is finding a parameterization for $C.$ In this lesson, we'll consider several different cases" :tutorial/id #uuid "ec010a30-571c-5ad5-b30a-c18ffdd997e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5823 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b85eebf-c70c-58a0-9476-5bcb73727ad3" :step/math-academy-id 35690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259787} {:db/id #ed